 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Short-circuit current limiting, shutdown on thermal overload, and compensation that keeps the output transistor inside its safe operating area are all internal; power dissipation is internally limited. The regulator needs no external parts to work."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Headroom",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Dropout is typically 1.1 V at 500 mA and 25 °C; the input stays about that far below the output even at the peak of its ripple. Input and output need a common ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitors",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The 0.33 µF input capacitor is needed only when the supply filter is some distance from the regulator. The 1.0 µF output capacitor improves stability and transient response."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection diodes",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "External capacitors can discharge into low-current nodes of the regulator or reverse the output polarity. A diode from output (anode) to ground (cathode) guards against output polarity reversal during start-up, power-off and short circuit. A second diode from input (anode) to output (cathode) keeps the output capacitor from discharging through the regulator when the input is shorted or the supply is switched off fast. With under 10 µF on the output, no protection diode is generally needed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DPAK copper area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "For a DPAK mounted vertically in free air on a square of 2.0 oz copper of side L, RθJA is about 92 °C/W at L = 0, 67 °C/W at 5 mm, 50 °C/W at 15 mm and 47 °C/W at 30 mm. PD(max) at TA = 50 °C rises from about 1.1 W at L = 0 to about 2.1 W at 30 mm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Internals",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The die holds 31 active transistors."
  }
 ],
